# Build-context denylist for the Python health-endpoint tier (port 8000).
#
# The sibling Dockerfile copies its runtime files by name, so this file does not
# decide what lands in the image -- it decides what the daemon has to receive
# before the build starts. Keep it an explicit denylist: a catch-all "*" with "!"
# re-inclusions is harder to reason about, and one mis-ordered negation drops a
# file silently.
#
# Never exclude pyproject.toml or config/health.json. health.py reads both at
# import and falls back to compiled-in literals rather than raising, and those
# literals are the same strings the manifest declares -- so an image built
# without them serves a byte-identical body from fallbacks and no reading of
# /health can tell the two apart. Only an existence check inside the image, or
# the "health configuration degraded" line server.py prints at start-up, can. So
# no broad *.py, *.toml, *.json, config or .python-version pattern.

# Version-control metadata. Here .git is a one-line gitdir POINTER file, because
# this tier is consumed as a submodule, so it carries no history -- but it does
# name a path inside the superproject's object store, and local remote-tracking
# configuration must never be baked into an image layer.
.git
.gitmodules
.gitignore

# Python execution residue from a local run. These files are machine-specific
# and are pure build-context weight: the Dockerfile copies named .py files and
# config/ only, so they could not reach the image in any case (and
# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 keeps the container from writing its own). The plain
# and "**/" forms are both listed because a leading segment without "**" matches
# at the context root only, so the second form is what reaches config/.
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
**/*.pyc

# Local environment files. A machine-local .env must never enter an image layer;
# the .env.* form generalizes that to every dotenv variant. .env.example is a
# documentation template and is named explicitly for the reader.
.env
.env.example
.env.*

# Documentation. Read from the repository, not from inside a container.
README.md

# Build-time-only container definitions: read from the context before the build,
# useless inside a running container.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# A .github directory, whenever one is added. Anything under it is read from the
# repository rather than from inside an image, so it never belongs in a context.
.github

# Test sources. A runtime image ships neither the tests nor the servers they
# start on ephemeral ports.
test_app.py

# The nested Java tier. It is a first-class part of this project and a TRACKED
# gitlink -- it must never appear in .gitignore -- but it builds its own image
# from its own Dockerfile and serves its own endpoint on port 8080.
nested_child_repo_10_LOC
